        "source_excerpt": "Mu'tazili arguments often resist making divine attributes real entities distinct from the divine essence, because multiplying eternal realities risks compromising tawhid. Anthropomorphic descriptions are often interpreted figuratively or as attributes/actions in relation to creation.\n\nAsh'ari arguments affirm real divine attributes while refusing crude anthropomorphism. This creates exam dilemmas around whether a student can affirm hearing, seeing, speech, power, and will without either multiplying gods or emptying scripture of meaning.\n\n## Created Qur'an / Divine Speech\n\nMu'tazili reasoning is associated with the view that the Qur'an as God's speech is created, because treating speech as co-eternal can look like adding another eternal beside",
